1
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:22,319
In 2025, NASA launched SPHEREx.

2
00:00:22,750 --> 00:00:24,141
Every six months,

3
00:00:24,141 --> 00:00:28,725
it maps the entire sky in 102 colours of infrared light.

4
00:00:29,150 --> 00:00:30,770
Again. And again.

5
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:34,301
And we couldn't stop asking:

6
00:00:34,301 --> 00:00:37,155
what if something important is hiding in there?

7
00:00:37,410 --> 00:00:39,899
We're Team Oblivion, from Bangladesh.

8
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:41,964
Tanvir Ahmed built the app.

9
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:44,418
Fahim Foysal Souwmik led the team.

10
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:46,925
Shefa Tabassum analysed the data.

11
00:00:46,930 --> 00:00:49,128
Sadman Sakib told our story.

12
00:00:49,130 --> 00:00:52,059
And Atahar Hossain Piash researched the science.

13
00:00:55,310 --> 00:00:57,059
And we built SPHEREx Explorer

14
00:00:57,059 --> 00:01:00,074
because we believe discovery shouldn't belong only

15
00:01:00,074 --> 00:01:02,932
to the people who have the tools to look for it.

16
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:08,938
Arizona, 1930. Clyde Tombaugh blinks

17
00:01:08,938 --> 00:01:12,642
between two photographs of the same patch of sky

18
00:01:12,710 --> 00:01:14,898
until one tiny point moves.

19
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:21,678
Almost a century later, the method hasn't changed.

20
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:27,473
Photograph the sky. Compare. Look for what moved.

21
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:37,598
The method is simple.

22
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:40,739
The scale isn't.

23
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:53,847
Somewhere inside all of this, something could be moving.

24
00:01:56,560 --> 00:01:59,136
The question is: will anyone notice?

25
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:05,330
So we built a new way to look.

26
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:08,900
Name any place in the sky.

27
00:02:08,900 --> 00:02:13,302
SPHEREx Explorer finds every image SPHEREx has taken of it,

28
00:02:13,302 --> 00:02:15,440
live from the public archive.

29
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:19,456
Then it does what Tombaugh did. It blinks the frames.

30
00:02:20,960 --> 00:02:22,478
And something moves.

31
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:27,276
Its own search finds the moving point,

32
00:02:27,276 --> 00:02:29,599
knowing nothing about asteroids.

33
00:02:29,599 --> 00:02:31,849
Then it checks with NASA's JPL.

34
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:36,992
It's the asteroid Iris, caught by SPHEREx in December 2025.

35
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:41,437
And it's honest. Each frame sees a different colour,

36
00:02:41,437 --> 00:02:43,895
so it never mistakes colour for change.

37
00:02:45,610 --> 00:02:48,995
It even knows where SPHEREx was, for every frame.

38
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:52,580
Seventy-five years of sky surveys, side by side.

39
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:56,498
A game that trains your eye. In English, and in Bangla.

40
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:01,932
Ask it anything. Every answer shows its sources.

41
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:11,822
For most of human history,

42
00:03:11,822 --> 00:03:14,729
the universe was something we could only look at.

43
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:23,657
Every generation built a better way to see.

44
00:03:24,280 --> 00:03:26,703
Our generation has something different.

45
00:03:27,680 --> 00:03:30,218
We have more data than ever before.

46
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:33,657
Now we need more people looking at it.

47
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:36,453
A student.

48
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:38,904
A citizen scientist.

49
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:40,391
An astronomer.

50
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:42,943
Anyone curious enough to look.

51
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:49,914
Because the next discovery may not be hiding from humanity.

52
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:53,484
It may simply be waiting for someone to notice.
